40625, 0.0100555419921875, -0.005741119384765625, 0.017852783203125, -0.008514404296875, 0.038299560546875, -0.0031261444091796875, -0.0177764892578125, -0.0107879638671875, -0.0123748779296875, -0.0032558441162109375, 0.0244903564453125, -0.00957489013671875, 0.0145416259765625, -0.0022144317626953125, 0.057464599609375, 0.01493072509765625, 0.0102996826171875, -0.0110015869140625, -0.00916290283203125, 0.0281982421875, -0.00461578369140625, 0.06005859375, 0.01512908935546875, 0.0294647216796875, -0.0024166107177734375, -0.0310821533203125, -0.0233917236328125, 0.04315185546875, 0.007495880126953125, 0.01422882080078125, 0.0168914794921875, 0.0257415771484375, -0.02703857421875, -0.023712158203125, -0.0157470703125, 0.03814697265625, 0.0278472900390625, -0.0222930908203125, 0.04559326171875, 0.01300048828125, 0.0183258056640625, -0.014923095703125, -0.0004215240478515625, 0.03753662109375, -0.004665374755859375, 0.02081298828125, 0.02081298828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The Great Barrier Reef: An Overview\n\nIntroduction  \nThe Great Barrier Reef is the world's largest coral reef system, composed of over 2,900 individual reefs and 900 islands. Stretching for more than 2,300 kilometres (1,400 miles) along the coast of Queensland, Australia, it covers an area of approximately 344,400 square kilometres (133,000 square miles). The reef lies in the Coral Sea, separated from the mainland by a channel up to 160 kilometres (100 miles) wide and over 61 metres (200 feet) deep in places. Its immense scale means that large portions of the reef can be observed from high altitudes, including from space in certain conditions, underscoring its vastness. The Great Barrier Reef is also notable as the world's largest single structure made by living organisms; billions of tiny coral polyps secrete calcium carbonate, gradually building the reef over thousands of years.\n\nEcology  \nEcologically, the Great Barrier Reef supports an extraordinary diversity of marine life, making it one of the most complex and important ecosystems on the planet. It is home to over 1,500 species of fish, such as the clownfish and the giant grouper, more than 400 species of hard and soft corals, and notable endangered species including the dugong and large green sea turtle. The reef also provides habitat for mollusks, sea snakes, and a variety of seabirds, contributing to its rich biodiversity.\n\nThreats  \nDespite its ecological significance, the Great Barrier Reef faces significant threats. Climate change has led to rising sea temperatures, resulting in mass coral bleaching events, such as those recorded in 2016 and 2017, which caused widespread coral mortality. Pollution from agricultural runoff introduces excess nutrients and sediments, harming coral health. Overfishing disrupts the balance of marine populations, while outbreaks of crown-of-thorns starfish further damage coral communities. These challenges have prompted urgent conservation efforts.\n\nConservation Efforts  \nOngoing initiatives aim to protect and preserve the reef\u2019s unique environment. The Great Barrier Reef Marine Park Authority (GBRMPA) manages the reef, implementing zoning plans to regulate activities and reduce human impact. Programs such as the Reef 2050 Plan coordinate government, scientific, and community actions to improve water quality, restore habitats, and monitor reef health. International organizations and local groups also contribute to research, education, and restoration projects.\n\nRecognition and Significance  \nThe Great Barrier Reef has received several significant recognitions. In 1981, it was designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site, acknowledging its outstanding universal value and the need for its protection. This status has helped attract global attention and funding for conservation. CNN named it one of the Seven Natural Wonders of the World in 1997, emphasizing its global significance and natural beauty, which draws millions of tourists annually and supports the regional economy. In 2006, the Queensland National Trust declared it a state icon of Queensland, reflecting its cultural and environmental importance to the region and reinforcing local stewardship.\n\nConclusion  \nThe Great Barrier Reef stands as a natural wonder of immense ecological, cultural, and economic value. Its preservation is urgent, requiring continued global and local efforts to ensure that this unique ecosystem endures for future generations."}}